00:32:59.420 --> 00:33:03.000
Then they do this very funny role play where Jim's like, get out of my office.

781
00:33:03.120 --> 00:33:04.500
It's like becomes a whole thing.

782
00:33:04.700 --> 00:33:10.440
But for real, the pitch Michael settles on is this one, and I thought we had to hear it.

783
00:33:10.620 --> 00:33:11.860
Sam, can you play it?

784
00:33:12.140 --> 00:33:15.820
Little girl in a field holding a flower.

785
00:33:16.060 --> 00:33:19.460
We zoom back to find that she's in the desert.

786
00:33:20.240 --> 00:33:21.960
And the field is an oasis.

787
00:33:22.340 --> 00:33:23.480
We zoom back further.

788
00:33:24.440 --> 00:33:29.040
The desert is a sandbox in the world's largest resort hotel.

789
00:33:29.320 --> 00:33:30.420
Zoom back further.

790
00:33:30.740 --> 00:33:36.800
The hotel is actually the playground of the world's largest prison.

791
00:33:37.720 --> 00:33:38.840
But we zoom back further.

792
00:33:39.140 --> 00:33:40.920
I can tell that your time is valuable.

793
00:33:41.060 --> 00:33:45.840
Oh, well, I love what he says when the guy is like, listen, I can tell your time is valuable.

794
00:33:45.960 --> 00:33:49.220
And Michael's like, well, actually, I don't get paid by the hour anymore.

795
00:33:49.400 --> 00:33:50.560
I get paid by the year.

796
00:33:50.820 --> 00:33:53.640
So, basically, I can waste as much time as I want.

797
00:33:54.020 --> 00:33:54.800
Not a problem.

798
00:33:54.800 --> 00:33:58.540
Yes, but I love that this is the pitch that he led with.

799
00:33:58.720 --> 00:34:02.560
And knowing that he had other ideas, but this is what he went with.

800
00:34:02.860 --> 00:34:04.300
The ad guys are great.

801
00:34:04.440 --> 00:34:06.900
They're so real in how they react to Michael.

802
00:34:07.140 --> 00:34:10.580
They say, listen, we made something for the Nashua branch.

803
00:34:10.740 --> 00:34:12.080
Why don't we show that to you?

804
00:34:12.240 --> 00:34:14.480
And it'll give you an idea of what we can do.

805
00:34:15.100 --> 00:34:16.840
So, he plays this commercial.

806
00:34:17.360 --> 00:34:23.160
And then they explain the only part of the commercial they're allowed to alter is the last five seconds.

807
00:34:23.460 --> 00:34:23.620
Yeah.

808
00:34:23.700 --> 00:34:24.360
And Michael's like, what?

809
00:34:24.360 --> 00:34:25.560
We just wave at the end?

810
00:34:25.720 --> 00:34:27.380
He goes, you know, you could clap.

811
00:34:27.520 --> 00:34:28.460
You know, make it your own.

812
00:34:28.620 --> 00:34:28.760
Yeah.

813
00:34:28.840 --> 00:34:30.660
This is where you can really get creative.

814
00:34:31.040 --> 00:34:32.280
You can be inside.

815
00:34:32.280 --> 00:34:33.300
You can be outside.

816
00:34:33.660 --> 00:34:35.080
We had a fan question.

817
00:34:35.460 --> 00:34:43.760
Aaron Jassa, Harry Driscoll, Molly Coates, and many others, they wanted to know who directed this commercial that plays in the middle of the episode.

818
00:34:43.760 --> 00:34:45.160
Was it Jason Reitman?

819
00:34:45.400 --> 00:34:47.460
Yes, it was Jason Reitman.

820
00:34:47.740 --> 00:34:55.620
I texted with BJ about this commercial, and he told me that Jason Reitman had directed real commercials before this.

821
00:34:55.760 --> 00:35:04.920
And he remembers that it was so cool because Jason laid out this big circular track on the floor and had the camera going around in circles.

822
00:35:05.100 --> 00:35:08.420
And BJ was like, I had never seen anything like that before.

823
00:35:08.640 --> 00:35:09.580
It was so cool.

824
00:35:09.580 --> 00:35:14.660
Well, we asked Jason about filming this fake commercial, and here's what he had to say.

825
00:35:15.320 --> 00:35:19.820
The episode Local Ad contained two commercials inside it, and I had been a commercial director.

826
00:35:19.960 --> 00:35:21.740
I was a bad commercial director, I should say.

827
00:35:21.940 --> 00:35:27.600
You know, the majority of the commercials that I directed were ones that if you saw, you would revoke my Director's Guild card.

828
00:35:27.600 --> 00:35:29.180
You would just say, you're not allowed to direct again.

829
00:35:29.320 --> 00:35:32.820
And then I would, you know, name clients, but that would be rude.

830
00:35:33.040 --> 00:35:39.200
But, you know, typical fast food and beer and just, but like, not good work.

831
00:35:39.200 --> 00:35:42.260
I was not Spike Jonze, but I learned that format.

832
00:35:42.700 --> 00:35:46.000
There's certain things that you may not know how to do authentically, but you know how to satirize.

833
00:35:46.000 --> 00:35:55.380
Like, I know that I could never direct a David Fincher film, but I love him so much as a filmmaker that I could probably direct a satire of a David Fincher film.

834
00:35:55.500 --> 00:36:00.200
And so, I don't know how to direct a great commercial, but I think I know how to direct the satire of a good commercial.

835
00:36:00.360 --> 00:36:06.900
And that was the kind of the great thing about doing the Dunder Mifflin ad, was I felt my commercial brain trying to do the best,

836
00:36:07.040 --> 00:36:12.120
worst version of what Dunder Mifflin would hand Michael Scott, which was this kind of cutesy commercial.

837
00:36:12.120 --> 00:36:15.880
It starred Jeff Witzke, who is an actor I've worked with since I was a kid.

838
00:36:15.980 --> 00:36:19.000
He literally was in my first short film and has been in everything I've ever made since.

839
00:36:19.100 --> 00:36:20.660
And he was the star of it, and that was great.

840
00:36:21.560 --> 00:36:25.560
I love that Jason just uses his friend Jeff in everything.

841
00:36:25.840 --> 00:36:26.940
I just think that's wonderful.

842
00:36:27.240 --> 00:36:29.700
Jenna, I want you to be in everything I do from here on out.

843
00:36:30.360 --> 00:36:30.800
Great.

844
00:36:31.400 --> 00:36:32.300
I love it.

845
00:36:32.600 --> 00:36:32.900
Okay.

846
00:36:33.680 --> 00:36:36.300
Well, we have some crossover connections with Jeff.

847
00:36:36.460 --> 00:36:37.120
Are you ready?

848
00:36:37.120 --> 00:36:43.940
He was also in Mindy and Brenda, the TV short written by Mindy Kaling and Brenda Withers.

849
00:36:44.220 --> 00:36:44.780
Oh.

850
00:36:45.020 --> 00:36:45.300
Yeah.

851
00:36:45.480 --> 00:36:46.960
He played the sales clerk.

852
00:36:47.340 --> 00:36:49.140
Well, lady, guess what?

853
00:36:50.060 --> 00:36:52.300
He also did a guest spot on Monk.

854
00:36:52.420 --> 00:36:56.280
He played Human Cannonball in Mr. Monk Goes to the Circus.

855
00:36:56.560 --> 00:36:57.700
That one really got me.

856
00:36:57.860 --> 00:36:59.480
I might need to look that one up.

857
00:36:59.640 --> 00:37:00.560
Human Cannonball.

858
00:37:00.760 --> 00:37:01.380
Very curious.

859
00:37:01.680 --> 00:37:04.000
You might need to see what the Human Cannonball looks like.

860
00:37:04.180 --> 00:37:04.600
Yes.

861
00:37:04.600 --> 00:37:10.060
So not only did Jason hire Jeff again, he also hired a friend that he had worked with

862
00:37:10.060 --> 00:37:12.180
before to compose that music.

863
00:37:12.180 --> 00:37:16.020
That sort of like, I don't know, it's like salsa music that's playing in the background.

864
00:37:16.020 --> 00:37:22.600
It was composed by the same composer that did all the music for Juno, Matteo Messina.

865
00:37:22.860 --> 00:37:24.080
I love that.

866
00:37:24.900 --> 00:37:27.180
Now things are going to get awkward.

867
00:37:27.660 --> 00:37:33.300
Andy pulls Dwight aside to get advice on how to get to first base with Angela.

868
00:37:33.300 --> 00:37:38.700
He says they've been necking a lot, but that means that they are literally rubbing their

869
00:37:38.700 --> 00:37:39.460
necks together.

870
00:37:39.740 --> 00:37:41.120
There's no kissing involved.

871
00:37:41.320 --> 00:37:43.240
Why don't we have any footage of that?

872
00:37:44.020 --> 00:37:45.420
I don't know.

873
00:37:45.860 --> 00:37:47.080
I don't know.

874
00:37:47.260 --> 00:37:50.200
Angela, we had a fan question from Amy Noki.

875
00:37:50.640 --> 00:37:55.440
She's dying to know, what is your backstory for Angela and Andy?

876
00:37:55.580 --> 00:37:57.080
And what is with the necking?

877
00:37:57.080 --> 00:38:02.100
Do you think this was standard for Angela, or is it because she can't stomach Andy and

878
00:38:02.100 --> 00:38:04.140
she's just trying to get back at Dwight?

879
00:38:04.340 --> 00:38:05.540
I love this question.

880
00:38:05.740 --> 00:38:10.080
I thought that Angela went out with Andy as sort of a revenge date.

881
00:38:10.340 --> 00:38:12.000
She was very mad at Dwight.

882
00:38:12.200 --> 00:38:14.140
She really thought she was done with Dwight.

883
00:38:14.260 --> 00:38:15.940
She was super, super angry with him.

884
00:38:16.040 --> 00:38:21.340
And as she started dating Andy, I think it became very clear to her that really Dwight had her

885
00:38:21.340 --> 00:38:24.220
heart, but she was too prideful to admit that.

886
00:38:25.000 --> 00:38:28.260
So she tried to date Andy, but it was a chore.

887
00:38:28.540 --> 00:38:32.840
And I think the necking thing is just something she was doing to put him off.

888
00:38:33.040 --> 00:38:36.620
Like, how can I let him touch me, but not really touch me?

889
00:38:36.980 --> 00:38:40.300
So yeah, I think her heart wasn't in it from the beginning.

890
00:38:40.960 --> 00:38:42.620
Yeah, I was thinking about it.

891
00:38:42.620 --> 00:38:50.400
And, you know, Angela can be very fussy and very structured, but we saw her going at it

892
00:38:50.400 --> 00:38:55.040
in Jim's little toy playhouse thing and email surveillance.

893
00:38:55.040 --> 00:39:00.660
So when she's into someone, it's not like she puts people through these weird paces of

894
00:39:00.660 --> 00:39:02.880
like, okay, first we just rub our necks together.

895
00:39:03.080 --> 00:39:05.220
I think this was exclusive to Andy.

896
00:39:05.480 --> 00:39:05.940
Oh, yeah.

897
00:39:06.220 --> 00:39:06.400
Yeah.

898
00:39:06.600 --> 00:39:08.040
And maybe the senator too.

899
00:39:08.340 --> 00:39:08.680
Perhaps.

900
00:39:08.800 --> 00:39:10.860
I really think Dwight was the love of her life.

901
00:39:10.860 --> 00:39:13.520
And, you know, it just took them a while to get there.

902
00:39:13.720 --> 00:39:14.560
Poor Dwight.

903
00:39:14.880 --> 00:39:16.780
He's having to listen to all this.

904
00:39:17.060 --> 00:39:19.200
And he tries to put Andy off.

905
00:39:19.360 --> 00:39:21.360
He says, I don't think we should talk about this.

906
00:39:21.500 --> 00:39:22.820
People might overhear us.

907
00:39:22.980 --> 00:39:27.440
But then Andy just takes that to mean that they should come up with code names.

908
00:39:28.460 --> 00:39:33.340
Dwight's like, okay, well, Angela can stay the same, but let's change Andy to Dwight.

909
00:39:33.740 --> 00:39:34.120
Hmm.

910
00:39:34.640 --> 00:39:34.960
Okay.

911
00:39:35.280 --> 00:39:35.500
Yeah.

912
00:39:35.640 --> 00:39:39.040
And Andy's like, that's not really, that's not how this works.

913
00:39:39.040 --> 00:39:46.860
Lady, this really reminded me of how you and I tried to come up with code names when we

914
00:39:46.860 --> 00:39:50.960
would have conversations and we were afraid other people could overhear us.

915
00:39:51.380 --> 00:39:54.240
And our code names were about this good.

916
00:39:54.440 --> 00:39:56.080
They were so obvious.

917
00:39:56.080 --> 00:39:58.540
It would be like a person's initials.

918
00:39:58.600 --> 00:39:59.780
So obvious.

919
00:40:00.160 --> 00:40:00.780
I know.

920
00:40:01.000 --> 00:40:03.020
We're not going to be spies, Jenna.

921
00:40:03.460 --> 00:40:08.100
We're not going to be spies, but we're going to be mom detectives solving small crimes.

922
00:40:08.100 --> 00:40:10.600
I would love to be a mom detective with you.

923
00:40:10.900 --> 00:40:15.720
Mom detectives solving the small crimes no one cares about in your neighborhood.

924
00:40:17.040 --> 00:40:18.360
It's a hit.

925
00:40:18.640 --> 00:40:22.220
Who graffitied the construction cone at the intersection?

926
00:40:23.100 --> 00:40:23.660
Exactly.

927
00:40:24.140 --> 00:40:25.320
We'll find out.

928
00:40:25.620 --> 00:40:26.700
We'll tell you.

929
00:40:26.880 --> 00:40:27.620
We're on it.

930
00:40:27.920 --> 00:40:32.180
So coming up, we have a scene between Ryan and Michael that is so funny to me.

931
00:40:32.880 --> 00:40:37.600
When Michael calls him, you know, to gripe, first of all, he calls him Rye.

932
00:40:39.400 --> 00:40:41.460
Ryan is like, don't call me about small probleMs.

933
00:40:41.600 --> 00:40:42.780
What have I told you about that?

934
00:40:43.240 --> 00:40:43.580
Yeah.

935
00:40:43.660 --> 00:40:46.240
And he says, well, you don't like it when I call you about big problems either.

936
00:40:46.520 --> 00:40:47.580
So what do you want from me?

937
00:40:47.620 --> 00:40:48.240
What do you want?

938
00:40:48.460 --> 00:40:52.740
So Jason said on the DVD commentary that they shot this in real time.

939
00:40:53.140 --> 00:40:57.940
Now, Jenna, do you remember they had built a little office that was Ryan's office at corporate,

940
00:40:58.160 --> 00:40:58.320
right?

941
00:40:58.320 --> 00:41:02.980
Yeah, so they had BJ, you know, on the other side of the soundstage in this little room

942
00:41:02.980 --> 00:41:04.280
with a camera with him.

943
00:41:04.480 --> 00:41:08.300
And then they had a camera with Steve in his office as Michael.

944
00:41:08.520 --> 00:41:10.440
And that's the room Jason sat in.

945
00:41:10.540 --> 00:41:11.920
And they shot it simultaneously.

946
00:41:12.420 --> 00:41:16.380
And I think it really works because the chemistry is happening in real time.

947
00:41:16.800 --> 00:41:21.020
I always loved when we did that because it meant that you could improvise a little.

948
00:41:21.220 --> 00:41:23.640
And the other person could react to you.

949
00:41:23.760 --> 00:41:25.680
And you would get it on both cameras.

950
00:41:26.000 --> 00:41:26.260
Yes.

951
00:41:26.260 --> 00:41:31.600
And BJ said something I thought was so delightful because it's one of my favorite lines in this

952
00:41:31.600 --> 00:41:32.300
whole episode.

953
00:41:32.560 --> 00:41:36.500
BJ said he wrote the line, I can't cook and I'm starting a restaurant.

954
00:41:36.880 --> 00:41:41.540
And the restaurant he says he's going to start is Mike's Cereal Shack.

955
00:41:41.680 --> 00:41:43.880
It's going to have every cereal you can get in the store.

956
00:41:44.180 --> 00:41:44.460
Yeah.

957
00:41:44.820 --> 00:41:49.680
I want you to know I did a little bit of a deep dive on Mike's Cereal Shack.

958
00:41:49.820 --> 00:41:50.720
I had to know.

959
00:41:50.860 --> 00:41:53.080
Has anyone opened a Cereal Shack?

960
00:41:53.080 --> 00:42:02.540
The answer is no, but a woman named Catherine has designed the most adorable vintage poster

961
00:42:02.540 --> 00:42:04.960
for Mike's Cereal Shack.

962
00:42:05.140 --> 00:42:09.120
She has a company on Etsy called Cat Bone Design.

963
00:42:09.120 --> 00:42:11.400
It's so, so cute.

964
00:42:11.400 --> 00:42:13.720
I'm going to put a picture of it in the pod.

965
00:42:13.720 --> 00:42:16.260
And then her profile really got me, Ange.

966
00:42:16.720 --> 00:42:22.200
Her profile says, I'm a stay-at-home mom with a passion for creating colorful and happy homes.

967
00:42:22.400 --> 00:42:25.360
Good design keeps me balanced and brings me joy.

968
00:42:25.600 --> 00:42:28.680
And being a designer means I can share that joy with others.

969
00:42:29.280 --> 00:42:30.400
Jenna shared it with me.

970
00:42:30.500 --> 00:42:31.160
It is adorable.

971
00:42:31.340 --> 00:42:34.680
We're going to put it in our stories, you guys, so you can look at the rest of her art.

972
00:42:35.080 --> 00:42:38.260
There's one more thing I want to say about this scene before we move on.

973
00:42:38.580 --> 00:42:39.500
Ryan's assistant.

974
00:42:39.800 --> 00:42:40.140
Yes.

975
00:42:40.140 --> 00:42:44.620
She pokes her head in the door at the beginning, and she says, Eddie Murphy is on the line for you.

976
00:42:44.880 --> 00:42:45.080
Yes.

977
00:42:45.420 --> 00:42:49.820
Well, Ryan's assistant was played by Virginia Newcomb, and I'll have you know,

978
00:42:50.020 --> 00:42:52.340
she has not been on an episode of Monk.

979
00:42:52.480 --> 00:42:52.980
I know.

980
00:42:53.080 --> 00:42:54.660
I looked to see if she was on Monk, too.

981
00:42:54.940 --> 00:42:57.440
But she has been on The Walking Dead.

982
00:42:57.840 --> 00:42:58.160
Oh.

983
00:42:58.340 --> 00:43:02.700
So no crossover connection, but perhaps you recognize her from The Walking Dead.

984
00:43:03.260 --> 00:43:04.760
So Michael says, you know what?

985
00:43:05.060 --> 00:43:06.260
Ad guys, go away.

986
00:43:06.400 --> 00:43:07.660
I'm going to do this on my own.

987
00:43:07.660 --> 00:43:10.440
He's going to stake his whole reputation on it.

988
00:43:10.660 --> 00:43:11.440
Oh, yeah.

989
00:43:11.740 --> 00:43:12.040
Yeah.

990
00:43:12.260 --> 00:43:13.720
He calls David Wallace.

991
00:43:14.080 --> 00:43:14.380
Yeah.

992
00:43:14.500 --> 00:43:19.040
And he says, listen, I'm going to make it.

993
00:43:19.420 --> 00:43:21.560
If you don't like it, fine.

994
00:43:21.560 --> 00:43:25.040
I'll bring those guys back, and I'll pay for it on my own dime.

995
00:43:25.160 --> 00:43:26.500
I need to do this.

996
00:43:26.780 --> 00:43:31.660
Well, he leads off the whole call with, I don't know who talks to their boss like this.

997
00:43:31.800 --> 00:43:33.560
I would never talk to a boss like this.

998
00:43:33.640 --> 00:43:36.300
He's like, hey, yeah, Ryan's being a little bitch again.

999
00:43:36.300 --> 00:43:39.160
And Ryan goes, I'm on the call, Michael.

1000
00:43:39.380 --> 00:43:40.320
And he's like, hey, bro.

1001
00:43:40.740 --> 00:43:42.280
Like, so awkward.

1002
00:43:42.640 --> 00:43:45.740
I just love Ryan's matter-of-factness.

1003
00:43:46.360 --> 00:43:49.700
He is never rattled by Michael being Michael.

1004
00:43:49.700 --> 00:43:53.140
And he's so direct and honest with Michael.

1005
00:43:53.140 --> 00:43:56.360
He is to Michael what Daryl is to Kelly.

1006
00:43:56.640 --> 00:43:57.120
Yes.

1007
00:43:57.360 --> 00:43:58.660
Just the straight truth.

1008
00:43:58.660 --> 00:44:02.060
And I feel like neither one of them knows how to handle that.

1009
00:44:02.860 --> 00:44:03.780
Not at all.

1010
00:44:04.200 --> 00:44:10.900
This scene with BJ as Ryan just being very direct reminded me the most of who BJ is in real life.

1011
00:44:11.560 --> 00:44:12.360
It's true.

1012
00:44:12.560 --> 00:44:15.600
And he's very fair, but he'll break it down for you.

1013
00:44:15.720 --> 00:44:17.940
He'll be like, I don't think you should do that, and here's why.

1014
00:44:18.120 --> 00:44:18.480
You know?

1015
00:44:18.480 --> 00:44:20.260
And it's what I love about him.

1016
00:44:20.480 --> 00:44:20.530
Me too.

1017
00:44:20.660 --> 00:44:25.060
Everybody needs that friend who doesn't blow smoke up your ass, frankly.

1018
00:44:25.460 --> 00:44:30.080
You need that person that you can call, and you know which friends are which friends.

1019
00:44:30.120 --> 00:44:33.320
You know the friends you call when you want them to agree with you.

1020
00:44:33.580 --> 00:44:37.340
And then you need that friend that you can call to get the straight truth.

1021
