It was not connected in this moment when we shot it, but that's how I think it might have

1264
00:56:05.500 --> 00:56:05.760
happened.

1265
00:56:05.760 --> 00:56:08.720
Yeah, I think the seed had been planted for Philly Jim.

1266
00:56:09.120 --> 00:56:10.180
Philly Jim.

1267
00:56:10.860 --> 00:56:12.520
And here's something crazy.

1268
00:56:12.620 --> 00:56:14.600
We'll get to it, but I'll bring it up here.

1269
00:56:14.780 --> 00:56:18.920
When he does move to Philly, there's an electric guitar in his apartment.

1270
00:56:19.740 --> 00:56:24.540
I don't know if he ever learns to play the guitar, but Philly Jim and his guitar, they

1271
00:56:24.540 --> 00:56:25.380
do show up again.

1272
00:56:25.660 --> 00:56:28.160
I mean, it was all connected, you guys.

1273
00:56:28.280 --> 00:56:28.960
I'm convinced.

1274
00:56:29.440 --> 00:56:34.440
Now at 12 minutes, 52 seconds, you guys, Daryl and his team are continuing to rehearse.

1275
00:56:34.440 --> 00:56:38.320
They've got a new theme song because, you know, Michael gave them notes, right?

1276
00:56:38.560 --> 00:56:40.420
So now they've written a new song.

1277
00:56:40.680 --> 00:56:42.660
And guess who wrote this version?

1278
00:56:43.200 --> 00:56:43.940
Tell us, Ange.

1279
00:56:44.040 --> 00:56:44.800
Craig Robinson.

1280
00:56:45.440 --> 00:56:45.940
Yes.

1281
00:56:46.400 --> 00:56:49.940
And of course, we knew that Craig was an amazing musician.

1282
00:56:50.380 --> 00:56:51.100
Oh, yeah.

1283
00:56:51.500 --> 00:56:52.360
Craig is fantastic.

1284
00:56:52.600 --> 00:56:53.780
He like tours and everything.

1285
00:56:53.960 --> 00:56:54.640
He's great.

1286
00:56:54.940 --> 00:56:59.600
In the DVD commentary, they shared that there was a heated debate in the writer's room about

1287
00:56:59.600 --> 00:57:02.080
which jingle they should use.

1288
00:57:02.080 --> 00:57:04.900
And they ultimately were like, wait, the room was divided.

1289
00:57:05.040 --> 00:57:06.040
They loved them both.

1290
00:57:06.120 --> 00:57:09.840
So they found a way to have them both in the episode, which I thought was great.

1291
00:57:10.200 --> 00:57:10.980
So fun.

1292
00:57:11.280 --> 00:57:16.020
Yeah, BJ told me it was cool because it set up this idea that Michael was really obsessive

1293
00:57:16.020 --> 00:57:18.580
and he kept making them write new versions of the song.

1294
00:57:19.200 --> 00:57:21.700
There's a line from this scene that I love from Michael.

1295
00:57:21.900 --> 00:57:23.480
He says, I hate it.

1296
00:57:23.540 --> 00:57:24.680
I don't hate it.

1297
00:57:24.760 --> 00:57:26.080
I just don't like it at all.

1298
00:57:26.240 --> 00:57:27.160
It's terrible.

1299
00:57:30.500 --> 00:57:34.540
One other thing I want to share with you that Craig talks about on the commentary is that

1300
00:57:34.540 --> 00:57:39.940
he only had an hour to rehearse with those guys and he said they got it right away.

1301
00:57:40.200 --> 00:57:40.780
Right away.

1302
00:57:40.920 --> 00:57:41.780
It just fell into place.

1303
00:57:42.100 --> 00:57:43.760
Except for maybe one moment.

1304
00:57:43.980 --> 00:57:45.100
We had a fan catch.

1305
00:57:45.280 --> 00:57:51.580
Rachel and Jeremy wrote in to say at 13 minutes, 15 seconds, Ed starts to sing at the wrong

1306
00:57:51.580 --> 00:57:53.400
time and then he almost breaks.

1307
00:57:53.640 --> 00:57:54.280
It's the beSt.

1308
00:57:54.900 --> 00:57:57.220
Lady, let's move to the parking lot.

1309
00:57:57.820 --> 00:58:01.300
Michael is really losing his patience with people.

1310
00:58:01.600 --> 00:58:04.600
Yeah, well, he says, you know, this is a pivotal scene.

1311
00:58:04.740 --> 00:58:07.200
If they don't get it, the whole thing will fall apart.

1312
00:58:07.660 --> 00:58:07.880
Yeah.

1313
00:58:08.340 --> 00:58:11.160
Andy is dressed in some sort of running gear.

1314
00:58:11.380 --> 00:58:15.920
They don't get very far because Phyllis comes back from the mall.

1315
00:58:16.080 --> 00:58:17.260
She is sobbing.

1316
00:58:17.400 --> 00:58:19.000
She is so gutted.

1317
00:58:19.140 --> 00:58:20.180
She is gutted.

1318
00:58:20.180 --> 00:58:24.780
She got kicked out of the mall in front of her friends.

1319
00:58:25.400 --> 00:58:27.920
All of her friends, I guess, love Sue Grafton.

1320
00:58:28.020 --> 00:58:29.680
They were all there for autographs.

1321
00:58:30.120 --> 00:58:34.320
And Phyllis just wouldn't take no for an answer because Michael told her, don't take no for

1322
00:58:34.320 --> 00:58:34.760
an answer.

1323
00:58:34.960 --> 00:58:36.220
Get Sue Grafton here.

1324
00:58:36.480 --> 00:58:39.160
And she kept bugging her and they kicked her out.

1325
00:58:39.460 --> 00:58:46.540
At the very end of the scene, there is a great little tag where Kevin offers Phyllis a piece

1326
00:58:46.540 --> 00:58:48.960
of tape in lieu of a tissue.

1327
00:58:48.960 --> 00:58:52.520
We got some mail about it, Angela, from Winston F.

1328
00:58:52.640 --> 00:58:53.820
and Delina Delano.

1329
00:58:54.060 --> 00:58:58.060
They wanted to know if this bit was improvised or scripted.

1330
00:58:58.220 --> 00:59:02.460
I texted Brian about it because I don't have the scripts for season four.

1331
00:59:02.700 --> 00:59:04.720
I'm going to guess it was improvised.

1332
00:59:04.900 --> 00:59:07.400
That seems very much like something Brian would improvise.

1333
00:59:07.800 --> 00:59:08.640
You are correct.

1334
00:59:09.000 --> 00:59:14.960
He told me that originally the scene had ended with the camera just lingering on Phyllis crying

1335
00:59:14.960 --> 00:59:18.600
and he was actually placed a little further in the background with Kate.

1336
00:59:18.600 --> 00:59:23.980
They were still kind of working on the little running scene, but he had the idea at the end

1337
00:59:23.980 --> 00:59:28.020
to walk up and offer her a piece of tape as a tissue.

1338
00:59:28.240 --> 00:59:29.580
Well, they loved it.

1339
00:59:29.780 --> 00:59:35.140
So the next time they did the scene, they actually pulled him a little bit more into the foreground

1340
00:59:35.140 --> 00:59:39.080
and they gave Steve the line, someone give her a tissue.

1341
00:59:39.080 --> 00:59:43.680
And then he tore off the piece of tape and that became the button of the scene.

1342
00:59:44.060 --> 00:59:45.260
It's so great.

1343
00:59:45.480 --> 00:59:46.140
It's perfect.

1344
00:59:46.520 --> 00:59:51.100
That's such a great example of how our improvisations would work into a scene.

1345
00:59:51.320 --> 00:59:52.440
We would do them.

1346
00:59:52.500 --> 00:59:58.460
And then if they liked them, they would say, okay, well, let's draw you into this in a way

1347
00:59:58.460 --> 01:00:01.760
that that creates a fun button or creates a fun moment.

1348
01:00:01.760 --> 01:00:06.140
I also want to point out, I think I can see Phyllis about to break.

1349
01:00:06.560 --> 01:00:09.400
She looks like she is literally about to laugh.

1350
01:00:09.560 --> 01:00:15.320
I don't know how to say this without it sounding weird, but I love every time the character of

1351
01:00:15.320 --> 01:00:16.340
Phyllis is crying.

1352
01:00:16.720 --> 01:00:17.320
Why?

1353
01:00:17.800 --> 01:00:20.400
Why is it so funny?

1354
01:00:20.760 --> 01:00:26.460
She's just so good at playing those emotional beats and I just love her.

1355
01:00:26.640 --> 01:00:27.760
I love it too.

1356
01:00:27.960 --> 01:00:28.940
I really love it.

1357
01:00:31.760 --> 01:00:38.860
At her desk, 14 minutes, 49 seconds, this Dunder Mifflin logo, she is just really putting

1358
01:00:38.860 --> 01:00:40.280
her heart and soul into it.

1359
01:00:40.440 --> 01:00:41.540
I admire her.

1360
01:00:41.720 --> 01:00:43.540
I think this is the key to success.

1361
01:00:43.820 --> 01:00:47.760
It doesn't matter how big or small the project, if you're getting paid or not getting paid,

1362
01:00:47.760 --> 01:00:51.300
when you're a struggling artist, you have to give every project your all.

1363
01:00:51.500 --> 01:00:55.200
She has to treat this as if it's going in a Martin Scorsese film.

1364
01:00:55.320 --> 01:00:56.360
And she is.

1365
01:00:56.440 --> 01:00:57.760
And I think she's right on.

1366
01:00:58.080 --> 01:00:58.540
Yes.

1367
01:00:58.680 --> 01:01:00.700
And so much so that she doesn't go home.

1368
01:01:00.700 --> 01:01:02.340
Jim's like, are you coming?

1369
01:01:02.720 --> 01:01:04.700
And she's like, no, I'm going to stay.

1370
01:01:04.940 --> 01:01:05.900
I have a question.

1371
01:01:05.940 --> 01:01:08.380
Is this a little hint that they're living together?

1372
01:01:09.020 --> 01:01:09.500
Interesting.

1373
01:01:09.820 --> 01:01:13.440
I never thought they were living together, but I think they're in that phase of their

1374
01:01:13.440 --> 01:01:16.840
relationship where they're staying over at each other's places a lot.

1375
01:01:17.040 --> 01:01:20.800
Like they might as well be living together, but I don't think they are yet.

1376
01:01:21.160 --> 01:01:21.480
Okay.

1377
01:01:21.660 --> 01:01:24.240
Well, poor Jim has to ride home now with Meredith.

1378
01:01:24.240 --> 01:01:25.180
Yes, he does.

1379
01:01:25.540 --> 01:01:31.940
And then Pam, we reveal, has slept in the office overnight.

1380
01:01:32.200 --> 01:01:39.480
And then the next morning opens with Pam waking up to a phone call from Jim.

1381
01:01:39.940 --> 01:01:41.080
He got her breakfaSt.

1382
01:01:41.420 --> 01:01:42.040
He got her breakfaSt.

1383
01:01:42.240 --> 01:01:45.700
And in the commentary, they said there was a big debate in the writer's room about,

1384
01:01:45.860 --> 01:01:48.100
does he bring her orange juice or coffee?

1385
01:01:48.100 --> 01:01:50.700
I don't remember seeing either on the desk.

1386
01:01:50.900 --> 01:01:55.680
There's a cup of orange juice and what looks like some kind of breakfast, maybe sandwich.

1387
01:01:56.120 --> 01:01:56.440
Yeah.

1388
01:01:56.620 --> 01:02:01.300
I'm guessing it's from McDonald's because Jim says that Creed ate the hash browns.

1389
01:02:01.380 --> 01:02:01.680
Yes.

1390
01:02:01.840 --> 01:02:07.280
But they felt like orange juice was just a sweeter token than coffee.

1391
01:02:07.560 --> 01:02:08.440
That's what they decided.

1392
01:02:08.620 --> 01:02:11.080
But no detail went unturned, you guys.

1393
01:02:11.080 --> 01:02:16.100
This is the point, that there was a whole conversation about, should there be orange juice or coffee?

1394
01:02:16.240 --> 01:02:18.740
That's how much thought was put into all of these moments.

1395
01:02:19.060 --> 01:02:22.120
Well, you know, Pam does not regularly drink coffee, you know.

1396
01:02:22.220 --> 01:02:23.060
She drinks tea.

1397
01:02:23.060 --> 01:02:25.260
So I think orange juice was the right choice.

1398
01:02:25.440 --> 01:02:25.620
Yeah.

1399
01:02:25.980 --> 01:02:30.820
Well, Pam explains in A Talking Head that she worked until 2.45 a.m.

1400
01:02:30.960 --> 01:02:35.440
and then she had to decide between spending the night with Michael and Dwight in the office

1401
01:02:35.440 --> 01:02:40.260
or risk driving home and falling asleep at the wheel and dying in a fiery car wreck.

1402
01:02:40.260 --> 01:02:42.800
She passed out on her keyboard trying to decide.

1403
01:02:43.100 --> 01:02:46.400
So this was the talking head with Jason that I could not get through.

1404
01:02:46.620 --> 01:02:48.800
I could not get through that last line.

1405
01:02:48.820 --> 01:02:53.620
I passed out on my keyboard trying to decide because there's this little pause.

1406
01:02:53.980 --> 01:02:57.980
And in that pause, the anticipation of that last line would make us laugh.

1407
01:02:58.280 --> 01:03:00.040
This is something you guys should all know.

1408
01:03:00.280 --> 01:03:03.180
I have found over the years, and I think, Jenna, you'll agree with me,

1409
01:03:03.360 --> 01:03:08.120
it's the pauses in comedy that get you almost more than the line.

1410
01:03:08.120 --> 01:03:12.400
When Steve would pause before saying something ridiculous as Michael,

1411
01:03:12.660 --> 01:03:16.240
it was that three-second pause that would have us all on the floor.

1412
01:03:16.380 --> 01:03:20.840
It was like this collective moment where we all knew what was coming, and we couldn't bear it.

1413
01:03:20.980 --> 01:03:22.220
A hundred percent.

1414
01:03:22.820 --> 01:03:23.800
I have a catch.

1415
01:03:24.180 --> 01:03:24.460
Okay.

1416
01:03:24.660 --> 01:03:28.840
In an upcoming episode, it is going to be revealed that Pam wears glasses.

1417
01:03:29.060 --> 01:03:32.200
Remember, because she's going to have to wear her spare pair to work.

1418
01:03:32.400 --> 01:03:32.640
Right.

1419
01:03:32.880 --> 01:03:35.620
Does that mean that Pam just slept in her contacts?

1420
01:03:36.120 --> 01:03:36.840
Can you do that?

1421
01:03:38.120 --> 01:03:39.400
But I think you're not supposed to do that.

1422
01:03:39.520 --> 01:03:40.740
I think that's a big no-no.

1423
01:03:40.840 --> 01:03:42.600
I think it dries your eyeballs out or something.

1424
01:03:42.920 --> 01:03:47.060
I mean, also, like, does Pam keep a toothbrush and toothpaste in her desk?

1425
01:03:47.120 --> 01:03:48.500
I thought the same thing.

1426
01:03:48.720 --> 01:03:51.440
And finally, before we move on from this little moment,

1427
01:03:51.960 --> 01:03:54.340
what has Dwight done to the couch?

1428
01:03:54.900 --> 01:03:59.880
He turned it on its side, and then he has taken all the cushions off,

1429
01:03:59.960 --> 01:04:02.120
and he is sleeping under the cushions.

1430
01:04:02.320 --> 01:04:03.880
It's so odd.

1431
01:04:03.880 --> 01:04:07.020
I think he got cold, and he was trying to make a cushion blanket.

1432
01:04:07.300 --> 01:04:08.420
I guess.

1433
01:04:08.700 --> 01:04:10.180
It's very strange.

1434
01:04:11.380 --> 01:04:13.160
I feel like he's done it before.

1435
01:04:13.160 --> 01:04:15.360
He has a system for that couch.

1436
01:04:15.640 --> 01:04:22.260
What we know now, Angela, is probably that Dwight, Pam, and Michael all have really bad breath,

1437
01:04:22.260 --> 01:04:25.720
because I can't imagine any of them had toothbrushes.

1438
01:04:25.880 --> 01:04:26.180
No.

1439
01:04:26.180 --> 01:04:27.600
Maybe Michael, though.

1440
01:04:27.800 --> 01:04:30.200
I feel like maybe Michael keeps one in his cabinet.

1441
01:04:30.240 --> 01:04:32.720
So he's got fresh breath, but I mean.

1442
01:04:32.860 --> 01:04:35.580
My guess is Michael has a whole change of clothes in that cabinet,

1443
01:04:35.600 --> 01:04:38.260
and he's got some boxer shorts, a pair of socks.

1444
01:04:38.560 --> 01:04:38.840
Yeah.

1445
01:04:38.960 --> 01:04:39.580
He's prepared.

1446
01:04:39.940 --> 01:04:41.900
Should we move into the break room?

1447
01:04:41.920 --> 01:04:44.240
Because Andy has some news for Dwight.

1448
01:04:44.380 --> 01:04:47.600
Oh, Operation Fallen Angel turned a corner.

1449
01:04:47.920 --> 01:04:51.160
I guess while they were doing their form of necking,

1450
01:04:51.160 --> 01:04:55.260
and he's kissing her earlobe, and she says, O-D.

1451
01:04:55.940 --> 01:04:58.760
Dwight's face lights up.

1452
01:04:59.080 --> 01:04:59.820
Lights up.

1453
01:04:59.980 --> 01:05:05.720
Andy thinks that this is because of his name, Andy, but we all know the truth.

1454
01:05:05.980 --> 01:05:07.400
Andy, what a stretch.

1455
01:05:07.820 --> 01:05:10.680
Why would she single out one letter of your name?

1456
01:05:10.840 --> 01:05:15.020
O-A-O-N-O-D-O-Y.

1457
01:05:15.500 --> 01:05:22.840
Yeah, but Dwight then has this very fun moment where he is rejoicing with Andy

1458
01:05:22.840 --> 01:05:25.820
over Angela calling out O-D.

1459
01:05:26.340 --> 01:05:27.400
There's hope, Dwight.

1460
01:05:27.760 --> 01:05:29.240
I love the next scene.

1461
01:05:29.440 --> 01:05:31.880
Michael sends the commercial to corporate.

1462
01:05:32.160 --> 01:05:33.760
He's like, they'll probably watch it right away.

1463
01:05:33.760 --> 01:05:34.620
I know I would.

1464
01:05:34.880 --> 01:05:35.160
Yeah.

1465
01:05:35.420 --> 01:05:36.580
And he's like, I'm not going to call him.

1466
01:05:36.680 --> 01:05:37.280
I'm not going to bug him.

1467
01:05:37.440 --> 01:05:39.500
And then he calls him 10 seconds later.

1468
01:05:39.720 --> 01:05:40.300
I know.

1469
01:05:40.420 --> 01:05:40.880
It's perfect.

1470
01:05:41.100 --> 01:05:41.980
We've all been there.

1471
01:05:42.140 --> 01:05:42.360
Yeah.

1472
01:05:42.360 --> 01:05:46.840
We've all sent somebody something we've written, something you've worked on.

1473
01:05:47.580 --> 01:05:49.840
And then you just wait and wait.

1474
01:05:50.020 --> 01:05:53.540
And then you do that thing in your head where you're like, oh my gosh, what if my email was

1475
01:05:53.540 --> 01:05:53.820
down?

1476
01:05:53.980 --> 01:05:54.120
Yeah.

1477
01:05:54.200 --> 01:05:55.220
What if they never got it?

1478
01:05:55.700 --> 01:05:56.900
Oh, I should call them.

1479
01:05:57.040 --> 01:05:58.860
I should text them and just nudge them.

1480
01:05:59.000 --> 01:05:59.120
Yeah.

1481
01:05:59.700 --> 01:06:00.820
10 days later.

1482
01:06:00.820 --> 01:06:01.960
And this was a big deal.

1483
01:06:02.060 --> 01:06:04.340
This kind of time jump we hadn't done in the show before.

1484
01:06:04.580 --> 01:06:05.580
It's very strange.

1485
01:06:05.580 --> 01:06:11.020
They had that little bit of an unwritten rule that the ideal episode happened over one

1486
01:06:11.020 --> 01:06:11.380
day.

1487
01:06:11.560 --> 01:06:11.780
Yeah.

1488
01:06:11.780 --> 01:06:13.160
We didn't always keep to it.

1489
01:06:13.280 --> 01:06:15.760
But for the most part, our episodes happened in one day.

1490
01:06:15.940 --> 01:06:17.500
So this says 10 days later.

1491
01:06:17.620 --> 01:06:18.600
It didn't bump me, Jenna.

1492
01:06:18.880 --> 01:06:19.160
No.

1493
01:06:19.360 --> 01:06:20.400
I was fine with it.

1494
01:06:20.600 --> 01:06:26.500
And Michael is saying, well, welcome to the world premiere of the Corporate Crap FeSt.

1495
01:06:27.200 --> 01:06:27.560
Yeah.

1496
01:06:27.740 --> 01:06:29.380
They did not pick his commercial.

1497
01:06:29.660 --> 01:06:30.460
They did not.

1498
01:06:30.680 --> 01:06:34.940
The whole gang has gathered at Poor Richard's Pub to watch the Corporate Crap FeSt.

1499
01:06:35.320 --> 01:06:35.700
Yeah.

1500
01:06:35.900 --> 01:06:36.060
Yeah.

1501
01:06:36.640 --> 01:06:37.560
They watch it.

1502
01:06:37.720 --> 01:06:38.540
They're delighted.

1503
01:06:38.880 --> 01:06:41.260
They are in the last five seconds.

1504
01:06:41.260 --> 01:06:43.820
It's the same commercial as the Nashua branch.

1505
01:06:43.980 --> 01:06:48.500
But this time it ends with everyone at Dunder Mifflin Scranton waving.

1506
01:06:49.000 --> 01:06:50.060
On the roof.

1507
01:06:50.680 --> 01:06:51.500
On the roof.

1508
01:06:51.620 --> 01:06:56.940
I guess that was Michael's big creative contribution is we'll wave from the roof.

1509
01:06:57.100 --> 01:06:57.260
Yeah.

1510
01:06:57.380 --> 01:06:58.580
We won't be in the parking lot.

1511
01:06:58.580 --> 01:06:59.900
We're going to go on the rooftop.

1512
01:07:00.860 --> 01:07:02.560
Jenna, do you remember I wasn't there that day?

1513
01:07:03.300 --> 01:07:03.740
No.

1514
01:07:04.080 --> 01:07:04.760
I know.

1515
01:07:05.080 --> 01:07:07.160
Mindy and I both were not there that day.

1516
01:07:07.800 --> 01:07:10.180
We had to be superimposed in.

1517
01:07:10.360 --> 01:07:12.040
We never went up on the roof.

1518
01:07:12.480 --> 01:07:14.100
We just did a wave.

1519
01:07:14.260 --> 01:07:16.420
They sort of cheated it in the parking lot.

1520
01:07:16.580 --> 01:07:20.620
And then they did a little fancy editing and put us in the shot.

1521
01:07:20.620 --> 01:07:27.120
That is crazy because I did look specifically to see if everyone was in there and you're there.

1522
01:07:27.300 --> 01:07:32.300
But if you look closely and I screen grabbed it, Mindy and I are kind of awkwardly off to

1523
01:07:32.300 --> 01:07:32.940
the side.

1524
01:07:33.180 --> 01:07:33.960
Do you see the gap?

1525
01:07:34.260 --> 01:07:35.580
That is crazy.

1526
01:07:36.060 --> 01:07:39.240
That's like that crazy thing they do when someone has to play their own twin.

1527
01:07:40.060 --> 01:07:40.460
Exactly.

1528
01:07:40.800 --> 01:07:43.140
Well, you can see it at 18 minutes, 10 seconds.

1529
01:07:43.780 --> 01:07:46.020
And I know why I couldn't be there that day.

1530
01:07:46.020 --> 01:07:52.740
I can't remember why Mindy couldn't be there, but I had one of my first OBGYN appointments

1531
01:07:52.740 --> 01:07:55.080
because I was pregnant.

1532
01:07:55.940 --> 01:08:02.620
Lady, I was wondering if you were going to share that in this episode because I just want

1533
01:08:02.620 --> 01:08:05.720
you to know I can see I'm getting goosebumps.

1534
01:08:06.380 --> 01:08:09.160
I can see your glow.

1535
01:08:09.460 --> 01:08:09.860
Really?

1536
01:08:10.240 --> 01:08:11.600
In this whole episode.

1537
01:08:11.880 --> 01:08:15.580
Yes, I clocked it the minute I put this episode on.

1538
01:08:15.580 --> 01:08:21.360
And I was like, oh my gosh, this is the episode where Angela was pregnant.

1539
01:08:21.780 --> 01:08:22.140
Yes.

1540
01:08:22.480 --> 01:08:25.280
And I had this appointment and I guess I couldn't move it.

1541
01:08:25.500 --> 01:08:27.180
And, you know, Kent was really sweet.

1542
01:08:27.320 --> 01:08:28.320
He was like, oh, it's fine, Angela.

1543
01:08:28.480 --> 01:08:29.240
Well, don't worry about it.

1544
01:08:29.280 --> 01:08:30.020
Go to your appointment.

1545
01:08:30.340 --> 01:08:31.900
Yeah, that's why I wasn't in it.

1546
01:08:32.020 --> 01:08:37.280
Well, I'll never forget, Angela, because you told me you were pregnant in my trailer at

1547
01:08:37.280 --> 01:08:37.720
work.

1548
01:08:37.880 --> 01:08:40.380
Yes, this is one of my favorite moments of our whole friendship.

1549
01:08:40.660 --> 01:08:43.220
We were in Jenna's trailer having lunch, as we often did.

1550
01:08:43.220 --> 01:08:45.540
And you were telling me a whole story.

1551
01:08:45.700 --> 01:08:47.140
I think it was about your cat.

1552
01:08:47.420 --> 01:08:48.480
Was it about your cat?

1553
01:08:48.760 --> 01:08:51.200
Lady, I remember I was done with lunch.

1554
01:08:51.200 --> 01:08:52.100
You were still eating.

1555
01:08:52.140 --> 01:08:57.500
And I was doing these little five-pound weights, little bicep curls.

1556
01:08:57.740 --> 01:09:00.380
Yes, you were telling me a whole story while lifting weights.

1557
01:09:00.400 --> 01:09:01.660
You're very passionate.

1558
01:09:01.660 --> 01:09:06.220
I'll never forget it because I go on and on and on and on.

1559
01:09:06.340 --> 01:09:12.820
I'm pumping my arms and then I get finished and you say, well, I have some news.

1560
01:09:13.240 --> 01:09:14.260
I'm pregnant.

1561
01:09:14.480 --> 01:09:18.120
And I just dropped the weights and I screamed.

1562
01:09:18.500 --> 01:09:23.340
And our AD came over to the trailer because I think they thought someone had had an accident.

1563
01:09:23.560 --> 01:09:26.280
There was this huge thud followed by a scream.

1564
01:09:26.280 --> 01:09:30.660
And then you said to me, Ange, why didn't you interrupt me?

1565
01:09:30.780 --> 01:09:32.980
I'm just rambling on about nothing.

1566
01:09:32.980 --> 01:09:35.560
And you're sitting on this big piece of information.

1567
01:09:35.780 --> 01:09:37.600
Why did you let me go on and on?

1568
01:09:37.720 --> 01:09:40.220
And I said, well, I wanted to hear your story.

1569
01:09:40.460 --> 01:09:42.480
My God, so funny.

1570
01:09:42.680 --> 01:09:44.060
I was just so happy for you.

1571
01:09:44.180 --> 01:09:45.820
I knew how much you wanted to be a mother.

1572
01:09:46.000 --> 01:09:51.580
And I could just, I'm tearing up right now thinking about it because you are such a good

1573
01:09:51.580 --> 01:09:51.900
mother.

1574
01:09:51.900 --> 01:09:57.100
You are a born nurturer and I was just so happy for you.

1575
01:09:57.760 --> 01:09:59.380
I knew how much you wanted that.

1576
01:10:01.020 --> 01:10:05.600
But yes, when I watched this episode, I knew right away, oh, this is the one.

1577
01:10:05.620 --> 01:10:07.380
And I didn't know if you were going to share it or not.

1578
01:10:07.400 --> 01:10:07.960
So I waited.

1579
01:10:08.520 --> 01:10:10.340
Yes, I was never up on the roof.

1580
01:10:10.520 --> 01:10:13.100
Never, never in the whole series.

1581
01:10:13.620 --> 01:10:14.020
Wow.

1582
01:10:14.180 --> 01:10:14.600
I know.

1583
01:10:14.760 --> 01:10:16.540
You're old hat at being up on the roof.

1584
01:10:16.660 --> 01:10:17.180
Oh, man.

1585
01:10:17.620 --> 01:10:19.340
So Jim does a very sweet thing.

1586
01:10:19.340 --> 01:10:25.020
He asked the bartender if he will play their commercial, the one that they all made together.

1587
01:10:25.220 --> 01:10:26.260
He hands them the disc.

1588
01:10:26.460 --> 01:10:28.880
And Michael is so moved by this.

1589
01:10:29.460 --> 01:10:29.680
Yeah.

1590
01:10:30.140 --> 01:10:34.140
Well, you know, the bartender slash waiter guy was played by Drew Powell.

1591
01:10:34.680 --> 01:10:37.420
You guys might recognize him from Gotham.

1592
01:10:37.560 --> 01:10:39.140
He's a regular on Gotham.

1593
01:10:39.240 --> 01:10:43.140
He also played Eddie Murdoch from the Monk episode, Mr. Monk Can't See a Thing.

1594
01:10:43.140 --> 01:10:46.020
And here's my crossover connection with Drew.

1595
01:10:46.280 --> 01:10:51.620
Drew and I played husband and wife in a Lifetime movie called Terror in the Woods.

1596
01:10:51.960 --> 01:10:53.280
Oh, I remember this.

1597
01:10:53.600 --> 01:10:55.820
Yes, we filmed it in Atlanta.

1598
01:10:55.880 --> 01:10:58.060
And so we hung out and spent some time together.

1599
01:10:58.240 --> 01:11:03.580
And when I got back to L.A., we realized our kids were at the same elementary school.

1600
01:11:03.960 --> 01:11:04.820
You're kidding me.

1601
01:11:04.920 --> 01:11:07.380
No, our sons went to elementary school together.

1602
01:11:07.580 --> 01:11:10.860
And then I started seeing him at morning assembly meetings and all kinds of stuff.

1603
01:11:11.100 --> 01:11:11.980
That's so crazy.

1604
01:11:11.980 --> 01:11:12.820
I know.

1605
01:11:13.080 --> 01:11:14.040
What a small world.

1606
01:11:14.280 --> 01:11:17.520
So everybody watches Michael's version of the commercial.

1607
01:11:17.560 --> 01:11:19.640
And it's so charming.

1608
01:11:20.140 --> 01:11:22.440
Everyone reacts really well to it.

1609
01:11:22.600 --> 01:11:28.200
And there was a decision to be made as to whether or not when we watch Michael's commercial,

1610
01:11:28.200 --> 01:11:31.620
we were going to be kind of embarrassed and like, oh, Michael.

1611
01:11:31.840 --> 01:11:33.760
Or if we were all going to love it.

1612
01:11:33.960 --> 01:11:35.600
And Jason talked about this.

1613
01:11:35.780 --> 01:11:39.340
It was really important to him that they pick the sweeter ending.

1614
01:11:39.580 --> 01:11:40.760
Here's what he had to say.

1615
01:11:40.760 --> 01:11:43.780
There was an opportunity for a tender ending.

1616
01:11:44.180 --> 01:11:51.600
And I got the sense that the writing staff of the office was hesitant to do too many of those.

1617
01:11:51.740 --> 01:11:55.180
That there's this kind of traditional television episode where it's funny throughout.

1618
01:11:55.420 --> 01:11:56.780
It has opportunity for satire.

1619
01:11:56.780 --> 01:12:00.900
And then towards the end, you have some sort of tender denouement.

1620
01:12:01.180 --> 01:12:03.040
And that they didn't want to be doing this every time.

1621
01:12:03.100 --> 01:12:04.980
But I really wanted that for this spot.

1622
01:12:05.100 --> 01:12:10.840
I really wanted the episode to end in a bar where the cast is watching the commercial together.

1623
01:12:10.840 --> 01:12:12.760
And they bond over it.

1624
01:12:12.880 --> 01:12:16.200
And there's something really lovely about people who come together to make something.

1625
01:12:16.360 --> 01:12:18.740
That was really important to me because that's an idea I know really well.

1626
01:12:18.840 --> 01:12:23.840
I think anyone who's ever done a high school play or made a high school video or done anything creative

1627
01:12:23.840 --> 01:12:28.680
knows what it feels like when outsiders come together to make something.

1628
01:12:29.180 --> 01:12:35.160
And no matter how it turns out, the collective process of creating something is just wonderful

1629
01:12:35.160 --> 01:12:41.020
and bonds you and erases fights because you can't help it but come together and make something.

1630
01:12:41.200 --> 01:12:43.000
And that's what the end of that episode felt like.

1631
01:12:43.020 --> 01:12:45.160
I remember everyone just standing in the bar watching the episode.

1632
01:12:45.260 --> 01:12:47.240
And we aired the actual, you know, we played back the actual thing.

1633
01:12:47.360 --> 01:12:48.300
It was just so lovely.

1634
01:12:48.300 --> 01:12:50.380
And I remember seeing a cut of the episode for the first time.

1635
01:12:50.500 --> 01:12:53.400
And again, watching an edit of the episode was much like directing it.

1636
01:12:53.540 --> 01:12:54.860
These guys knew what they were doing.

1637
01:12:55.000 --> 01:12:57.000
You know, I don't think I had any notes.

1638
01:12:57.000 --> 01:13:01.280
I just kind of watched an office episode that I happened to have a directing credit on.

1639
01:13:01.400 --> 01:13:02.820
And it has a slightly emotional ending.

1640
01:13:02.880 --> 01:13:07.360
And I'm really proud and I'm really grateful that the writing staff was up for that at the

1641
01:13:07.360 --> 01:13:10.320
end of the day because it makes me cherish the episode even more.

1642
01:13:11.000 --> 01:13:11.900
I love it too.

1643
01:13:12.240 --> 01:13:13.300
Oh my gosh.

1644
01:13:13.480 --> 01:13:15.600
Yeah, I couldn't imagine it any other way.

1645
01:13:15.600 --> 01:13:20.140
I mean, this was such a big collective project that everyone came together for.

1646
01:13:20.240 --> 01:13:24.320
I feel like everyone in the office would have had some kind of personal interest in it.

1647
01:13:24.360 --> 01:13:27.400
And I'm so glad we were happy and reacted that way.

1648
01:13:27.540 --> 01:13:28.780
I have a confession.

1649
01:13:29.100 --> 01:13:29.420
What?

1650
01:13:29.760 --> 01:13:34.920
The very beginning of the commercial, it starts with a woman's hand taking a piece of paper

1651
01:13:34.920 --> 01:13:36.060
off of the copier.

1652
01:13:36.320 --> 01:13:36.460
Yeah.

1653
01:13:36.740 --> 01:13:38.460
That is supposed to be me.

1654
01:13:38.660 --> 01:13:39.600
That's supposed to be Pam.

1655
01:13:39.760 --> 01:13:41.380
But I wasn't there that day.

1656
01:13:41.560 --> 01:13:42.440
I don't know why.

1657
01:13:42.440 --> 01:13:45.260
I was not pregnant, everybody, for whatever reason.

1658
01:13:45.600 --> 01:13:49.720
They put Pam's watch and Pam's sweater on someone else.

1659
01:13:49.880 --> 01:13:50.720
I don't know.

1660
01:13:50.920 --> 01:13:54.600
I kind of feel like it was one of our ADs or one of our PAs.

1661
01:13:54.820 --> 01:13:56.860
So that is not my hand at the top.

1662
01:13:57.040 --> 01:13:58.200
I did not know that.

1663
01:13:58.380 --> 01:13:59.580
I am not in this either.

1664
01:13:59.940 --> 01:14:03.980
One of the things Jason said in the commentary about it that I loved is that he said filming

1665
01:14:03.980 --> 01:14:07.900
this really reminded him of home movies he would make with his dad.

1666
01:14:08.040 --> 01:14:11.080
His dad is Ivan Reitman, an amazing director.

1667
01:14:11.080 --> 01:14:16.880
Jason said when he was a kid, he had a little handheld camera, you know, and he would make

1668
01:14:16.880 --> 01:14:20.800
these videos like the paper airplane like that, all of that with his dad.

1669
01:14:21.100 --> 01:14:23.100
And it really reminded him of that.

1670
01:14:23.200 --> 01:14:24.660
And that's, in fact, how they shot.

1671
01:14:24.780 --> 01:14:26.060
They shot with just a little handheld.

1672
01:14:26.060 --> 01:14:31.180
It's funny you mentioned the paper airplane because we had a fan question from Grant Pickle,

1673
01:14:31.280 --> 01:14:34.320
who said, how did you get the shot of the paper airplane?

1674
01:14:34.680 --> 01:14:39.240
Well, guys, they just taped it to the front of this little handheld camera.

1675
01:14:39.440 --> 01:14:42.800
And Jason talked about the inspiration behind that shot.

1676
01:14:43.180 --> 01:14:44.280
Sam, will you play that clip?

1677
01:14:44.760 --> 01:14:50.240
There was a shot that really became popular in the 90s and you saw it in like Sam Raimi

1678
01:14:50.240 --> 01:14:51.960
films and John Woo filMs.

1679
01:14:52.220 --> 01:14:56.920
I associate it with Evil Dead 2 and Army of Darkness, but also would have been popular

1680
01:14:56.920 --> 01:15:01.220
in like literally any John Woo film where you like follow a bullet, you know, where it

1681
01:15:01.220 --> 01:15:04.840
looks like the camera is riding on the back of a bullet or an arrow or something like

1682
01:15:04.840 --> 01:15:05.040
that.

1683
01:15:05.120 --> 01:15:09.520
And I could have just imagined, you know, Michael Scott seeing any one of these films,

1684
01:15:09.520 --> 01:15:13.560
you know, Mission Impossible 2 or like Broken Arrow or, you know, I don't know.

1685
01:15:13.680 --> 01:15:16.620
I don't know if Michael Scott watches the old Hong Kong films, but maybe he just saw like

1686
01:15:16.620 --> 01:15:20.920
Van Damme and Hard Target and was like, we need to do a camera attached to the paper airplane

1687
01:15:20.920 --> 01:15:21.240
shot.

1688
01:15:21.460 --> 01:15:23.460
And again, this is my favorite kind of thing.

1689
01:15:23.600 --> 01:15:26.160
It would be kind of great to make an entire movie that way.

1690
01:15:29.140 --> 01:15:31.360
I love how much thought went into this commercial.

1691
01:15:31.520 --> 01:15:32.400
It's so perfect.

1692
01:15:32.660 --> 01:15:32.960
Yeah.

1693
01:15:33.280 --> 01:15:35.940
We end this episode with a little tag.

1694
01:15:36.320 --> 01:15:37.980
It's Andy's talking head.

1695
01:15:37.980 --> 01:15:42.640
And he's still trying to figure out the end of the Kit Kat jingle.

1696
01:15:42.980 --> 01:15:44.920
He cannot figure it out.

1697
01:15:45.060 --> 01:15:47.360
And he struggles with it throughout the whole episode.

1698
01:15:47.620 --> 01:15:50.760
Jenna, I wrote down every single guess that Andy made.

1699
01:15:51.040 --> 01:15:55.200
Ed also mentions in the commentary that he must have improvised like 20 of these.

1700
01:15:55.360 --> 01:16:00.040
And he didn't know until he watched the episode which ones made it in.

1701
01:16:01.180 --> 01:16:05.400
Here's all of the wrong guesses Andy makes throughout this episode.

1702
01:16:05.400 --> 01:16:15.460
Break me off a piece of that applesauce, Chrysler car, football cream, lumber tar, Snickers bar,

1703
01:16:16.160 --> 01:16:27.900
gray Poupon, Claude Van Damme, hair for men, poison gas, NutraSweet, and finally, fancy feaSt.

1704
01:16:27.900 --> 01:16:35.720
And he's convinced, that's it, I did it, nailed it, it's a cat food, break me off a piece of that fancy feaSt.

1705
01:16:36.860 --> 01:16:38.060
So, good.

1706
01:16:38.400 --> 01:16:41.960
Before we go, we have a final message from Jason.

1707
01:16:42.540 --> 01:16:48.060
You know, my daughter, Josie, is 14 and she was a baby when I directed The Office.

1708
01:16:48.860 --> 01:16:52.960
And to this day, she has only seen one movie I've directed.

1709
01:16:53.080 --> 01:16:55.740
In fact, up until recently, she hadn't seen any film I'd ever directed.

1710
01:16:55.860 --> 01:17:01.440
And the only thing she had ever seen that I had directed was The Office, of which she is a super fan.

1711
01:17:01.660 --> 01:17:05.020
And that made her overwhelmingly proud of me.

1712
01:17:05.020 --> 01:17:08.700
And it made me very grateful that I had directed The Office.

1713
01:17:09.100 --> 01:17:11.320
Because it had put me in such high esteem of my child.

1714
01:17:11.520 --> 01:17:16.960
But just to say thank you, it really is a privilege to be part of the history of that show.

1715
01:17:17.100 --> 01:17:18.600
Every once in a while, it'll pop up in a way.

1716
01:17:18.780 --> 01:17:21.500
Certainly when I'm doing interviews, it's one of the questions that always comes up.

1717
01:17:21.560 --> 01:17:22.860
What was it like to direct The Office?

1718
01:17:23.000 --> 01:17:26.560
Because it's kind of like, you know, there's only so many people who have walked on the moon.

1719
01:17:26.620 --> 01:17:28.300
And there's only so many people who have directed The Office.

1720
01:17:28.480 --> 01:17:30.240
And I get to be one of those people.

1721
01:17:30.380 --> 01:17:31.880
I've directed two episodes, in fact.

1722
01:17:31.880 --> 01:17:35.080
And it's something that I carry really proudly.

1723
01:17:35.280 --> 01:17:37.320
Because, you know, I didn't create that thing.

1724
01:17:37.400 --> 01:17:38.520
I just got to be a gueSt.

1725
01:17:38.720 --> 01:17:42.860
You know, there's this presumption that you need to be angry to do comedy.

1726
01:17:43.220 --> 01:17:47.100
And being at The Office proves that concept wrong.

1727
01:17:47.540 --> 01:17:51.660
Because it was a group of some of the loveliest and funniest human beings I've ever met.

1728
01:17:52.140 --> 01:17:53.000
So sweet.

1729
01:17:53.140 --> 01:17:53.900
So sweet.

1730
01:17:54.340 --> 01:17:57.320
And Josie, we're so glad we helped make your dad cool.

1731
01:17:57.800 --> 01:17:58.100
Yeah!

1732
01:17:58.500 --> 01:17:59.380
There you go!

1733
01:18:00.300 --> 01:18:03.160
Guys, thank you so much again for sending in your questions.

1734
01:18:03.320 --> 01:18:05.160
And Jason, thanks for sending in all those clips.

1735
01:18:05.420 --> 01:18:08.920
And we will be here next week talking about Branch Wars.

1736
01:18:09.580 --> 01:18:11.080
It's another hidden gem.

1737
01:18:11.320 --> 01:18:13.700
I think we're going to have a few hidden gems in a row.

1738
01:18:14.300 --> 01:18:15.280
I'm predicting.

1739
01:18:15.660 --> 01:18:16.800
See you next week, everybody.

1740
01:18:17.300 --> 01:18:22.420
Thank you for listening to Office Ladies' Second Drink.

1741
01:18:22.660 --> 01:18:26.360
This episode was initially created in collaboration with Earwolf.

1742
